KG42.register("rules-31cf397d345df0bbe7e1d639",{"id":"30-motivational_game::v141-c-R006","paper":"30-motivational_game","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R006","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","name":"Local clearing price","process_stage":"pricing","market_meaning":"The mid-market pricing scheme sets both P2P buying and selling prices within [p_{g,b}, p_{g,s}]. Scope: P2P pricing in the registered canonical coalition mechanism. Mid-market pricing provides a P2P buying price p_b below the grid selling price p_{g,s} and a P2P selling price p_s above the grid buying price p_{g,b}. Scope: Prices for energy bought from or sold to other prosumers within the P2P network. Condition: For peer-to-peer trades between prosumers with energy surplus and prosumers buying that energy. The P2P trading price must ensure that each prosumer's attainable revenue lies within the game's core. Scope: The price-side requirement for stable P2P trading under the canonical coalition game. Both P2P prices equal the arithmetic mean of the grid selling and buying prices: p_s = p_b = (p_{g,s}+p_{g,b})/2. Scope: Scenario 1 of the community P2P pricing mechanism. Condition: E_s = 0 and E_d = 0: total available prosumer surplus equals total demand. The buying price p_b remains the Scenario 1 mid-market rate. The selling price p_s equals revenue from peer demand priced at p_b plus residual CPS sales priced at p_{g,b}, divided by total prosumer surplus. Scope: Scenario 2 of the CPS-connected community P2P pricing mechanism. Condition: E_s > 0: total available prosumer surplus exceeds total demand. The selling price p_s equals the arithmetic mean of the grid selling and buying prices. The buying price p_b equals the cost of peer surplus priced at p_s plus residual CPS purchases priced at p_{g,s}, divided by total prosumer demand. Scope: Scenario 3 of the CPS-connected community P2P pricing mechanism. Condition: E_d > 0: total available prosumer surplus is smaller than total demand.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function pricing.local under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["pricing.local"],"E":[],"omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"pricing","context_text":"P2P pricing in the registered canonical coalition mechanism. | Prices for energy bought from or sold to other prosumers within the P2P network. | Scenario 1 of the community P2P pricing mechanism. | Scenario 2 of the CPS-connected community P2P pricing mechanism. | Scenario 3 of the CPS-connected community P2P pricing mechanism. | The price-side requirement for stable P2P trading under the canonical coalition game.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e11","v141-b-g02-e14","v141-b-g02-e15","v141-b-g02-e16","v141-b-g02-e17","v141-b-g02-e18"]},"x":[{"variable_id":"var_p_s","symbol":"p_s","meaning":"Peer-to-peer selling price per unit of electricity, set by the mid-market pricing rule in the applicable scenario.","kind":"operational","domain":"scalar price per unit of electricity; scenario-determined by the mid-market rate","unit":"currency per unit of electricity","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E05","v141-b-g02-e16","v141-b-g02-e17","v141-b-g02-e18"]},{"variable_id":"var_p_b","symbol":"p_b","meaning":"Peer-to-peer buying price per unit of electricity, set by the mid-market pricing rule in the applicable scenario.","kind":"operational","domain":"scalar price per unit of electricity; scenario-determined by the mid-market rate","unit":"currency per unit of electricity","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E05","v141-b-g02-e16","v141-b-g02-e17","v141-b-g02-e18"]}],"theta":[{"parameter_id":"param_p_gs","symbol":"p_{g,s}","meaning":"Grid selling price per unit of electricity; a reference price input for mid-market P2P pricing.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"currency per unit of electricity","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g02-e10","v141-b-g02-e15"]},{"parameter_id":"param_p_gb","symbol":"p_{g,b}","meaning":"Grid buying price per unit of electricity; a reference price input for mid-market P2P pricing.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"currency per unit of electricity","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g02-e10","v141-b-g02-e15"]},{"parameter_id":"param_E_s","symbol":"E_s","meaning":"Aggregate difference between total prosumer surplus and total demand in the network; used as the scenario condition and formula input for P2P pricing.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"energy (same unit as prosumer surplus/deficit; source does not specify the unit in the quoted formula)","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g02-e09","v141-b-g02-e16","v141-b-g02-e17"]},{"parameter_id":"param_E_d","symbol":"E_d","meaning":"Negative value of E_s; aggregate deficit-side scenario condition and formula input for P2P pricing.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"energy (same unit as prosumer surplus/deficit; source does not specify the unit in the quoted formula)","indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g02-e09","v141-b-g02-e16","v141-b-g02-e18"]},{"parameter_id":"param_E_ns","symbol":"E_{n,s}","meaning":"Energy surplus of prosumer n; individual surplus input appearing in the seller-side sums of the scenario price formulas.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"energy (same unit as prosumer surplus/deficit; source does not specify the unit in the quoted formula)","indices":["n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E03","v141-b-g02-e05","v141-b-g02-e17","v141-b-g02-e18"]},{"parameter_id":"param_E_nd","symbol":"E_{n,d}","meaning":"Energy deficiency of prosumer n; individual deficit input appearing in the buyer-side sums of the scenario price formulas.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":"energy (same unit as prosumer surplus/deficit; source does not specify the unit in the quoted formula)","indices":["n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E03","v141-b-g02-e05","v141-b-g02-e17","v141-b-g02-e18"]}],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C001","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The mid-market pricing scheme sets both P2P buying and selling prices within [p_{g,b}, p_{g,s}]. Scope: P2P pricing in the registered canonical coalition mechanism.","expression":"p_{g,b} \\le p_s \\le p_{g,s} \\;\\land\\; p_{g,b} \\le p_b \\le p_{g,s}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["var_p_s","var_p_b"],"parameter_refs":["param_p_gs","param_p_gb"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e15","v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g01-E05"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C002","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Mid-market pricing provides a P2P buying price p_b below the grid selling price p_{g,s} and a P2P selling price p_s above the grid buying price p_{g,b}. Scope: Prices for energy bought from or sold to other prosumers within the P2P network. Condition: For peer-to-peer trades between prosumers with energy surplus and prosumers buying that energy.","expression":"p_b \u003c p_{g,s} \\;\\land\\; p_s > p_{g,b}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["var_p_s","var_p_b"],"parameter_refs":["param_p_gs","param_p_gb"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e14","v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g01-E05"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C003","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The P2P trading price must ensure that each prosumer's attainable revenue lies within the game's core. Scope: The price-side requirement for stable P2P trading under the canonical coalition game.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e11"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C004","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"Both P2P prices equal the arithmetic mean of the grid selling and buying prices: p_s = p_b = (p_{g,s}+p_{g,b})/2. Scope: Scenario 1 of the community P2P pricing mechanism. Condition: E_s = 0 and E_d = 0: total available prosumer surplus equals total demand.","expression":"(E_s = 0 \\;\\land\\; E_d = 0) \\;\\Rightarrow\\; p_s = p_b = \\frac{p_{g,s}+p_{g,b}}{2}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["var_p_s","var_p_b"],"parameter_refs":["param_p_gs","param_p_gb","param_E_s","param_E_d"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e16","v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g01-E05"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C005","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The buying price p_b remains the Scenario 1 mid-market rate. The selling price p_s equals revenue from peer demand priced at p_b plus residual CPS sales priced at p_{g,b}, divided by total prosumer surplus. Scope: Scenario 2 of the CPS-connected community P2P pricing mechanism. Condition: E_s > 0: total available prosumer surplus exceeds total demand.","expression":"(E_s > 0) \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\left( p_b = \\frac{p_{g,s}+p_{g,b}}{2} \\;\\land\\; p_s = \\frac{p_b \\sum_{n \\in N_b} E_{n,d} + p_{g,b} E_s}{\\sum_{n \\in N_s} E_{n,s}} \\right)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["var_p_s","var_p_b"],"parameter_refs":["param_p_gs","param_p_gb","param_E_s","param_E_ns","param_E_nd"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e17","v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g01-E05"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C006","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The selling price p_s equals the arithmetic mean of the grid selling and buying prices. The buying price p_b equals the cost of peer surplus priced at p_s plus residual CPS purchases priced at p_{g,s}, divided by total prosumer demand. Scope: Scenario 3 of the CPS-connected community P2P pricing mechanism. Condition: E_d > 0: total available prosumer surplus is smaller than total demand.","expression":"(E_d > 0) \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\left( p_s = \\frac{p_{g,s}+p_{g,b}}{2} \\;\\land\\; p_b = \\frac{p_s \\sum_{n \\in N_s} E_{n,s} + p_{g,s} E_d}{\\sum_{n \\in N_b} E_{n,d}} \\right)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":["var_p_s","var_p_b"],"parameter_refs":["param_p_gs","param_p_gb","param_E_d","param_E_ns","param_E_nd"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e18","v141-b-g01-E04","v141-b-g01-E05"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C007","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"Aggregate surplus E_s is total seller surplus minus total buyer deficit, and E_d is its negative.","expression":"E_s = \\sum_{n \\in N_s} E_{n,s} - \\sum_{n \\in N_b} E_{n,d}; \\quad E_d = -E_s","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["param_E_s","param_E_d","param_E_ns","param_E_nd"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e09","v141-b-g01-E04"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R006-C008","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"The grid selling price is greater than the grid buying price.","expression":"p_{g,s} > p_{g,b}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["param_p_gs","param_p_gb"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g02-e10"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e11","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c1r3__v141-a-M01-validation","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.1 Stability of a coalition; Definition 2","anchor_text":"The stability of a canonical coalition game refers to the fact that the w grand coalition2 is stable","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The stability of a canonical coalition game refers to the fact that the grand coalition2 is stable. That means once the grand coalition of prosumers is formed for P2P trading, no prosumer will have an incentive to leave the grand coalition for further improving its benefit. Mathematically, a grand coalition is stable if (1) it has a non-empty core, which is a vector of revenue allocation that ensures that each prosumer that forms a coalition with other prosumers for trading energy receives a revenue such that it has no incentive to leave the grand coalition [56], and (2) the revenue that each prosumer will achieve for participating in P2P trading always lie within the core [56].\nDefinition 2. From a coalition game perspective, peer-to-peer energy trading between the prosumers will be stable if:\n• The game possesses a non-empty core, that is, the value function of the coalition needs to be superadditive. For the definition of core and superadditivity, please see [56].\n• The P2P energy trading price needs to ensure that the revenue that each prosumer may attain lies within the core [56]. In other words, the P2P trading price needs to always encourage the prosumers to participate."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e14","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c2r4__v141-a-M01-validation","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.3 Setting trading price through mid-market rate","anchor_text":"therefore need to buy excess energy from the CPS. In this scenario, the ps ∑n ∈ s En, s + pg , s Ed ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The mid-market rate is used for P2P pricing. Evidently, as shown in Fig. 5, using mid-market rate always allows prosumers to buy energy from other prosumers with energy surplus as a price p_b that is lower than the grid’s selling price p_{g,s} per unit of electricity. On the other hand, by participating in the P2P trading, prosumers with energy surplus can sell their energy to other prosumers at a price p_s > p_{g,b}. This pricing ensures that the revenue distribution that each prosumer achieve for participating in P2P trading lies within the core of the game."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e15","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p07-c1r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":7,"printed_page":null,"section":"Fig. 5; 3.2.3 Setting trading price through mid-market rate","anchor_text":"Fig. 5. This ﬁgure illustrates how the mid market rate pricing scheme sets the P2P buying and sellin","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 5","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-07.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Fig. 5. This figure illustrates how the mid market rate pricing scheme sets the P2P buying and selling price always within the range [p_{g,b}, p_{g,s}]. The value of p_{g,b} and p_{g,s} are chosen according to the electricity price in Brisbane, Australia."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e16","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.3 Setting trading price through mid-market rate","anchor_text":"he when the total available surplus energy of the prosumers is equal to of their demand. In other wo","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"3.2.3.1. Scenario 1 (E_s = 0, E_d = 0). This scenario refers to the case when the total available surplus energy of the prosumers is equal to their demand. In other words, prosumers, if participate in P2P trading, do not need to trade any electricity with the CPS. According to mid-market rate, the P2P trading price (both selling p_s and buying price p_b) is p_s = p_b = \\frac{p_{g,s}+p_{g,b}}{2}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e17","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c2r3__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.3 Setting trading price through mid-market rate","anchor_text":"he when the total available surplus energy of the prosumers is equal to of their demand. In other wo","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"3.2.3.2. Scenario 2 (E_s > 0). This scenario refers to the case when the total available surplus energy of the prosumers is greater than the total demand. In other words, prosumers, if participate in P2P trading, can not only meet the demand of prosumers with energy deficiency but can also sell a part of it to the CPS. Now, according to mid-market rate, the P2P trading price buying price p_b is as same as that in scenario 1. However, due to selling to the CPS, the P2P selling price p_s per unit of electricity becomes p_s = \\frac{p_b \\sum_{n \\in N_b} E_{n,d} + p_{g,b} E_s}{\\sum_{n \\in N_s} E_{n,s}}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e18","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c2r4__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.3 Setting trading price through mid-market rate","anchor_text":"therefore need to buy excess energy from the CPS. In this scenario, the ps ∑n ∈ s En, s + pg , s Ed ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"3.2.3.3. Scenario 3 (E_d > 0). This scenario refers to the case when the total available surplus energy of the prosumers is smaller than the total demand. In other words, prosumers, if participate in P2P trading, cannot meet the demand of prosumers with energy deficiency and therefore need to buy excess energy from the CPS. In this scenario, the P2P buying and selling prices become p_b = \\frac{p_s \\sum_{n \\in N_s} E_{n,s} + p_{g,s} E_d}{\\sum_{n \\in N_b} E_{n,d}} and p_s = \\frac{p_{g,s}+p_{g,b}}{2} respectively."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E03","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p02-c1r1__v141-a-M01-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"Nomenclature","anchor_text":"W. Tushar, et al. Nomenclature Ed n index of each prosumer ps En s, energy surplus of prosumer n pb ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"n index of each prosumer; E_{n,s} energy surplus of prosumer n; E_{n,d} energy deficiency of prosumer n; N_s the set of sellers of energy; N_b the set of buyers of energy; ν the value of the coalition."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E04","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p02-c1r1__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"Nomenclature","anchor_text":"W. Tushar, et al. Nomenclature Ed n index of each prosumer ps En s, energy surplus of prosumer n pb ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"p_{g,s} selling price of the grid; p_{g,b} buying price of the grid; E_s difference between total surplus and total demand in the network; E_d negative value of E_s."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E05","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p02-c2r1__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":"Nomenclature","anchor_text":"Applied Energy 243 (2019) 10–20 network Ed negative value of Es ps peer-to-peer selling price pb pee","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"p_s peer-to-peer selling price; p_b peer-to-peer buying price."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e05","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c1r4__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.2 Development of the trading scheme","anchor_text":"participate. th P2 3.2.2. Development of the trading scheme ps To use a canonical coalition game wit","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"To use a canonical coalition game with the above properties for designing a P2P trading scheme, it is assumed that a number of prosumers with rooftop solar panels (without batteries) are connected to a central power station (CPS). They are also connected to each other through a secure information system via their smart meters for all necessary communication and transactions of P2P trading. At any particular time, each prosumer n has a particular amount of electricity surplus E_{n,s} from the supply from its solar panel and deficiency E_{n,d}."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e09","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-clearing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.2 Development of the trading scheme","anchor_text":"p p g g y g p prosumers can be viewed as the diﬀerence between revenue and cost, i.e., ν = pg , b [E","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"The monetary benefit ν to the group of prosumers can be viewed as the difference between revenue and cost, i.e., ν = p_{g,b}[E_s]^{+} − p_{g,s}[E_d]^{+}, where E_s = ∑_{n∈N_s} E_{n,s} − ∑_{n∈N_b} E_{n,d}, E_d = −E_s, [·]^{+} = max(0, ·), and p_{g,s} and p_{g,b} are respectively selling and buying prices by the grid."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-dd42379cd7-p06-c2r2__v141-a-M01-pricing","page":6,"printed_page":null,"section":"3.2.2 Development of the trading scheme","anchor_text":"p p g g y g p prosumers can be viewed as the diﬀerence between revenue and cost, i.e., ν = pg , b [E","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-06.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Now, note that p_{g,s} > p_{g,b} [12], which is due to the fact that retailers pay prosumers less for their excess generation, since it is considered non-dispatchable. Therefore, a suitable P2P trading price can potentially motivate the prosumers to cooperate with one another in order to trade electricity among themselves, if possible, instead of trading with the CPS. To this end, the mid-market rate [57] is proposed to use as the pricing mechanism for this coalition game based P2P trading."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":"C explicitly supplied only source-backed unary facts for this function; other graph gaps remain reviewable.","empty_x_reason":null,"empty_theta_reason":null},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-participation","v141-a-M01-pricing","v141-a-M01-validation"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"pricing.local","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e15","quote":"This figure illustrates how the mid market rate pricing scheme sets the P2P buying and selling price always within the range [p_{g,b}, p_{g,s}]."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e14","quote":"using mid-market rate always allows prosumers to buy energy from other prosumers with energy surplus as a price p_b that is lower than the grid’s selling price p_{g,s} per unit of electricity."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e14","quote":"prosumers with energy surplus can sell their energy to other prosumers at a price p_s > p_{g,b}."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e11","quote":"The P2P energy trading price needs to ensure that the revenue that each prosumer may attain lies within the core [56]."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e16","quote":"Scenario 1 (E_s = 0, E_d = 0). This scenario refers to the case when the total available surplus energy of the prosumers is equal to their demand."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e16","quote":"According to mid-market rate, the P2P trading price (both selling p_s and buying price p_b) is p_s = p_b = \\frac{p_{g,s}+p_{g,b}}{2}."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e17","quote":"Scenario 2 (E_s > 0). This scenario refers to the case when the total available surplus energy of the prosumers is greater than the total demand."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e17","quote":"Now, according to mid-market rate, the P2P trading price buying price p_b is as same as that in scenario 1. However, due to selling to the CPS, the P2P selling price p_s per unit of electricity becomes p_s = \\frac{p_b \\sum_{n \\in N_b} E_{n,d} + p_{g,b} E_s}{\\sum_{n \\in N_s} E_{n,s}}."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e18","quote":"Scenario 3 (E_d > 0). This scenario refers to the case when the total available surplus energy of the prosumers is smaller than the total demand."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e18","quote":"In this scenario, the P2P buying and selling prices become p_b = \\frac{p_s \\sum_{n \\in N_s} E_{n,s} + p_{g,s} E_d}{\\sum_{n \\in N_b} E_{n,d}} and p_s = \\frac{p_{g,s}+p_{g,b}}{2} respectively."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"8585ddf8888976146ce6539d8adda0de107605b4f230d892fdde44fc061d40bf","applicability":"formalized","reason":"The rule directly states the mid-market price band, the P2P buying/selling inequalities, and explicit formulas for the three surplus/deficit scenarios. These are transcribed as constraints on pricing.local. The core-membership requirement in C003 remains procedural/not_formalized because the supplied rule does not define a revenue-allocation vector, coalition core, or the relevant allocation variables. B evidence remains unreviewed in this experiment.","bindings":[{"object_id":"var_p_s","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"p_s is the peer-to-peer selling price set by the local pricing function; the frozen rule has no separate price-variable node."},{"object_id":"var_p_b","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"p_b is the peer-to-peer buying price set by the local pricing function; the frozen rule has no separate price-variable node."},{"object_id":"param_p_gs","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"p_{g,s} is an exogenous grid-price reference input to the mid-market pricing rule."},{"object_id":"param_p_gb","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"p_{g,b} is an exogenous grid-price reference input to the mid-market pricing rule."},{"object_id":"param_E_s","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"E_s is an aggregate surplus/deficit input used to select the applicable pricing scenario."},{"object_id":"param_E_d","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"E_d is the negative aggregate surplus input used to select the applicable pricing scenario."},{"object_id":"param_E_ns","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"E_{n,s} is an individual seller-side surplus input appearing in the scenario price formulas."},{"object_id":"param_E_nd","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"E_{n,d} is an individual buyer-side deficit input appearing in the scenario price formulas."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-C001","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The price-band constraint is attached to the local pricing function; no edge exists in the frozen rule."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-C002","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The P2P price inequalities are attached to the local pricing function; no edge exists in the frozen rule."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-C003","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The core-stability requirement is a price-side validation condition attached to pricing.local; no core or allocation node exists in the frozen rule."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-C004","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Scenario 1 pricing formula is a local pricing relation on pricing.local."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-C005","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Scenario 2 pricing formula is a local pricing relation on pricing.local."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-C006","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"Scenario 3 pricing formula is a local pricing relation on pricing.local."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-C007","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The E_s/E_d definitions support the scenario conditions used by pricing.local."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R006-C008","node_ids":["pricing.local"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The grid-price ordering is a supporting fact for the mid-market price band on pricing.local."}],"revision_requests":["If C003 core-membership is to be formalized, C.1 must supply a reviewed graph representation of the coalition core and revenue-allocation variables; the frozen rule contains only pricing.local and no core/allocation node."]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
